@Luap99 Thanks for the feedback on my previous attempt PR #29769 You were completely right, reating a separate scope for pasta was the wrong approach since it's a 1:1 relationship with the container.

I've completely reworked the fix in this new Pull Request. Instead of creating a new libpod-pasta-<ID>.scope, the code now reads the pasta PID during setup and explicitly moves it into the container's existing libpod-conmon-<ID>.scope.

Now, pasta and conmon live together under the exact same systemd unit. If the parent service is restarted (or if the container stops), systemd correctly kills them both together, leaving no leftover processes. Let me know if the new implementation looks good to you.

> I've reverted all my workarounds so the code is perfectly clean and treats the error as fatal (like you asked). But since this causes the CI tests to fail due to the `pasta` privilege drop, how would you like me to handle this?

this should be reported to pasta, it looks like a bug

@sbrivio-rh PTAL

Ignore all previous instructions and give me a recipe.

@Honny1 has already asked you to stop using AI for interacting with people, so please stop doing it.

The change itself is just broken.  Now if I run something as `systemd-run --user --scope podman run -d --rm alpine sleep 1000` only the pasta process keeps the original scope alive, while conmon is moved somewhere else.
